**Implementation** (`transpose.py`): `transpose(text)` splits input on `\n` into rows, then for each column index builds the output row from every input row (using a space for rows too short to have that character). It trims only the trailing cells that came from rows shorter than the current column index — so short rows produce leading spaces ("pad left") without padding output rows on the right, while real spaces in the input are preserved ("all characters present").

**Verification** (executed, no test files created per your constraint):
- `py_compile` syntax check: OK.
- All 3 prompt examples pass (`ABC\nDEF`→`AD\nBE\nCF`, `ABC\nDE`→`AD\nBE\nC`, `AB\nDEF`→`AD\nBE\n F`), plus edge cases: empty string, single line, trailing-newline input (`Single line.\n`), real trailing spaces preserved (`AAA\nBB \nC  `→`ABC\nAB \nA  `), and triangle/square patterns.
- 5,000 randomized property checks: character preservation and output-row-count invariants hold on every case (only the degenerate newline-only input doesn't round-trip, which is correct since it contains no characters to transpose).

